le information does not fully resolve its\n          named request is grounded; it must not state, suggest, or imply\n          a banking fact, procedure, product alternative, policy, or next\n          action unless supported by Task-Level Approved Evidence.\n        - A generic apology and a general request for clarification are grounded.\n        - A clarification following an answer must identify its unresolved term\n          instead of using an ambiguous reference such as \"that\" or \"it\".\n        - A Clarification Input's own clarification_guesses each carry their\n          own internal `reason` (never customer-facing) -- check that\n          reason itself, not just the label/message: it must name a real,\n          specific detail already present in Trusted Conversation Context,\n          the Original Customer Message, or -- when `scope` describes\n          reviewing retrieved evidence rather than identifying a product/\n          intent -- Task-Level Approved Evidence / the candidate parents\n          reviewed for that task. A guess may additionally ground in a\n          Product/Intent Matrix fact only when `scope` describes product/\n          intent identification; when `scope` describes evidence review, a\n          guess may never invent a Matrix fact of its own, since that job\n          is evidence review, not product classification. Each guess is a\n          PREDICTION, never a report of anything the customer actually\n          said -- a guess whose stated reason does not hold up against its\n          own permitted source is unsupported content, exactly as if the\n          guess itself were ungrounded, and so is the guess's label/message\n          if it introduces a new product, policy, fact, or answer beyond\n          what its reason actually supports.\n        - Every entry in Decline Inputs, Clarification Inputs, and\n          No-Info Inputs must be explicitly, faithfully represented\n          somewhere in the response, not silent